              "text": "The word **hosanna** has a rich, dual meaning that serves as both a **plea for help** and a **shout of praise and adoration**. \n\nHere is a breakdown of its definition, origin, and how its meaning has evolved:\n\n### 1. Literal Translation (Etymology)\nThe word comes from the Hebrew phrase *hoshi'ah-na* (\u05d4\u05d5\u05b9\u05e9\u05b4\u05c1\u05d9\u05e2\u05b8\u05d4\u05be\u05e0\u05b8\u05bc\u05d0). \n* **Hoshi'ah** means \"save\" or \"rescue\".\n* **Na** is a particle meaning \"please\" or \"we pray.\"\n* Therefore, the literal translation of hosanna is **\"Save, please!\"** or **\"Save, we pray!\"**\n\nIt was transliterated into Greek as *h\u014dsanna* (\u1f61\u03c3\u03b1\u03bd\u03bd\u03ac), then into Latin, and eventually into English.\n\n### 2. Meaning in Judaism\nIn the Hebrew Bible, the word is used as a direct cry to God for rescue or salvation. Its most famous biblical origin is in **Psalm 118:25**, which says, *\"Lord, save us [hoshi'ah-na]; Lord, grant us success.\"* \n\nOver time, this plea became integrated into Jewish liturgy. During the festival of *Sukkot* (the Feast of Tabernacles), worshippers recite prayers for deliverance known as *Hoshanot* while waving branches.\n\n### 3. Meaning in Christianity\nIn the New Testament, the meaning of the word shifted from a desperate plea for help to a **cry of joyful praise, acclamation, and triumph**. \n\nDuring Jesus's triumphal entry into Jerusalem on Palm Sunday, crowds waved palm branches and shouted, *\"Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest heaven!\"* (Matthew 21:9). \n* In this context, the crowd was recognizing Jesus as the Messiah.\n* Their shout was a proclamation: *\"Save us now, Messiah, our King!\"* combined with *\"Glory and praise to our Savior!\"*\n\n### Summary of Modern Usage\nToday, \"hosanna\" is used primarily in Christian worship, hymns, and liturgy (such as the *Sanctus* or \"Holy, Holy, Holy\" prayer in Catholic and liturgical Protestant services). \n\nWhen someone says or sings \"hosanna\" today, they are using it as an **expression of praise and adoration to God**, celebrating the belief that He is the one who saves.",
